WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.375
Welcome to CSC-244.

2
00:00:03.625 --> 00:00:08.125
In this lesson, you’ll create Kotlin objects with constructor properties, copy

3
00:00:08.125 --> 00:00:12.625
a data object, and implement an interface with an explicit override.

4
00:00:12.875 --> 00:00:16.775
Constructor properties give each object its starting data.

5
00:00:17.025 --> 00:00:21.800
An interface names an operation that an implementing class must provide.

6
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.369
These skills help separate an app’s information from the behavior that uses

7
00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:27.750
it.

8
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.500
A clear data model keeps related values together.

9
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:36.645
A shared interface lets different implementations offer the same operation, so

10
00:00:36.645 --> 00:00:40.650
callers do not need to know every implementation detail.

11
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:44.575
Imagine you’re developing an Android notes app.

12
00:00:44.825 --> 00:00:47.950
A note has a text title and an archived flag.

13
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.050
The flag is Boolean: it records whether the note is archived.

14
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.825
Our sample title is Lab ideas.

15
00:00:55.075 --> 00:01:00.182
We want an archived version of this note while keeping the original object

16
00:01:00.182 --> 00:01:00.575
available.

17
00:01:01.575 --> 00:01:06.342
We’ll describe the note data, define a formatting operation, and create a

18
00:01:06.342 --> 00:01:08.725
formatter that returns a note’s title.

19
00:01:08.975 --> 00:01:13.750
Then we’ll make a changed copy and inspect the title and both archive flags.

20
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.050
That comparison will show which object each value belongs to.

21
00:01:19.151 --> 00:01:22.776
Let’s open demo.kts and build the note model.

22
00:01:23.026 --> 00:01:27.890
The standard Run Code button will execute this script with the installed

23
00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:28.701
Kotlin compiler.

24
00:01:28.953 --> 00:01:33.486
The title and archived state belong to the same note, so we’ll keep them

25
00:01:33.486 --> 00:01:35.428
together in a Note data class.

26
00:01:35.678 --> 00:01:38.953
Its main job is to carry these related values.

27
00:01:39.203 --> 00:01:44.203
The data modifier also supplies useful operations, including copy.

28
00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:54.740
The parameter list after Note is its primary constructor.

29
00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:59.813
Putting val before title and archived makes them properties of each new

30
00:01:59.813 --> 00:02:00.215
object.

31
00:02:00.465 --> 00:02:03.490
The constructor receives their starting values.

32
00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:08.390
Archived has false as its default when the caller omits that argument.

33
00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.140
These properties are public and readable through the object, but val provides

34
00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.890
no setter for assigning a different value to either property.

35
00:02:17.142 --> 00:02:21.767
To represent a changed version, we can ask copy to create another Note.

36
00:02:22.017 --> 00:02:26.856
Copy starts with the existing constructor-property values, then replaces only

37
00:02:26.856 --> 00:02:28.792
the arguments we name.

38
00:02:29.042 --> 00:02:31.642
It does not rewrite the original object.

39
00:02:31.892 --> 00:02:37.209
This is a shallow copy: if a property held another mutable object, both copies

40
00:02:37.209 --> 00:02:39.867
could refer to that same nested object.

41
00:02:40.117 --> 00:02:43.317
Here the properties hold only text and a Boolean.

42
00:02:43.567 --> 00:02:47.092
Work through a separate example before our archive test.

43
00:02:47.342 --> 00:02:52.267
Suppose a note has the title Draft and uses the default archive flag.

44
00:02:52.517 --> 00:02:55.967
Copy it with only the title changed to Study plan.

45
00:02:56.217 --> 00:03:00.142
The original still has Draft, and the new note has Study plan.

46
00:03:00.392 --> 00:03:05.442
Both keep the original archive flag because that property was not replaced.

47
00:03:05.692 --> 00:03:10.367
This separates creating a changed object from editing an existing one.

48
00:03:10.618 --> 00:03:14.493
The app also needs a way to turn a note into display text.

49
00:03:14.743 --> 00:03:19.643
We’ll define a NoteFormatter interface that promises a format operation.

50
00:03:19.893 --> 00:03:24.396
The interface describes what a formatter accepts and returns, without choosing

51
00:03:24.396 --> 00:03:26.443
how it creates the text.

52
00:03:31.991 --> 00:03:36.391
The format function accepts a Note and promises a String result.

53
00:03:36.641 --> 00:03:38.816
There is no function body here.

54
00:03:39.066 --> 00:03:44.412
An implementing class must supply that behavior, which lets a caller rely on

55
00:03:44.412 --> 00:03:47.291
the same operation even when formatters differ.

56
00:03:47.542 --> 00:03:51.692
For this version of the app, the display text should be just the title.

57
00:03:51.942 --> 00:03:56.965
We’ll make TitleFormatter implement the interface and provide that specific

58
00:03:56.965 --> 00:03:57.467
behavior.

59
00:04:05.955 --> 00:04:09.130
The colon names the interface being implemented.

60
00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:14.357
Override explicitly marks format as the implementation of the promised

61
00:04:14.357 --> 00:04:14.855
operation.

62
00:04:15.105 --> 00:04:18.805
Its expression body returns the note’s title property.

63
00:04:19.055 --> 00:04:22.280
That property access uses the dot after note.

64
00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:26.580
The caller gets text back; the formatter does not change the note.

65
00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:31.798
Kotlin requires the override marker, which makes this relationship visible to

66
00:04:31.798 --> 00:04:33.605
the reader and compiler.

67
00:04:33.857 --> 00:04:36.232
The model and formatter are ready.

68
00:04:36.482 --> 00:04:41.501
Now create the sample original, request a copy with a different archive flag,

69
00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:43.432
and create a formatter object.

70
00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:48.257
Keep separate names for the two notes so we can inspect them independently.

71
00:04:57.512 --> 00:05:02.162
The Note call supplies Lab ideas and leaves archived at its default.

72
00:05:02.412 --> 00:05:06.487
The copy call supplies true for the copy’s archived argument.

73
00:05:06.737 --> 00:05:09.837
TitleFormatter needs no input arguments.

74
00:05:10.087 --> 00:05:14.521
Remember our separate title-copy example: properties omitted from the copy

75
00:05:14.521 --> 00:05:18.512
call keep their existing values in the new object.

76
00:05:18.762 --> 00:05:21.712
We’ll use that rule to trace this archive change.

77
00:05:21.964 --> 00:05:26.514
Let’s print the formatter’s result and label each note’s archive flag.

78
00:05:37.443 --> 00:05:40.143
Before running, predict all three lines.

79
00:05:40.393 --> 00:05:42.793
Which title will the formatter return?

80
00:05:43.043 --> 00:05:46.118
Which object does each labeled flag come from?

81
00:05:46.368 --> 00:05:51.268
Pause and trace the constructor defaults and the one named copy argument.

82
00:05:57.521 --> 00:06:01.896
Now run the script and compare the actual output with your prediction.

83
00:06:10.713 --> 00:06:16.173
The first line is Lab ideas because copying the archive flag kept the title,

84
00:06:16.173 --> 00:06:18.513
and our formatter returns that title.

85
00:06:18.763 --> 00:06:20.938
Original archived is false.

86
00:06:21.188 --> 00:06:23.113
Copy archived is true.

87
00:06:23.363 --> 00:06:28.299
Those labels confirm that we created a changed object while the original kept

88
00:06:28.299 --> 00:06:29.438
its starting state.

89
00:06:29.688 --> 00:06:33.438
The formatter read the archived copy without modifying it.

90
00:06:33.689 --> 00:06:37.914
We’ve connected a data model with a separate behavior contract.

91
00:06:38.164 --> 00:06:42.754
Constructor properties keep each note’s values together, copy creates a

92
00:06:42.754 --> 00:06:48.262
related version, and the interface with an explicit override defines how this

93
00:06:48.262 --> 00:06:49.639
formatter supplies text.

94
00:06:49.889 --> 00:06:54.214
The actual output connects each value back to the correct object.

95
00:06:54.465 --> 00:06:58.390
Now model a reminder with a title and a completion flag.

96
00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:03.633
Make a copy with a changed completion value, and write a formatter through an

97
00:07:03.633 --> 00:07:03.990
interface.

98
00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:07.190
Print both objects’ values to check your work.

99
00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.762
What results would show that your copy changed the intended property while the

100
00:07:12.762 --> 00:07:13.990
original stayed available?
